<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">
  <changeSet author="GA-Lotse" id="1744121614001-1">
    <addColumn tableName="person">
      <column name="human_readable_id" type="BIGINT"/>
    </addColumn>
  </changeSet>
  <changeSet author="GA-Lotse" id="1744121614001-2">
    <!-- Here we want to generate random, but unique human-readable IDs for each reference person.
    In the database, the human-readable IDs are stored as numbers from 0 to 2^40-1. In order to make
    sure that the IDs are unique, we calculate them from the "normal" ID which is simply an incrementing
    number, but in such a way that the output looks random and doesn't make it feasible to calculate the
    original ID from a human-readable ID. So we first add a random number between 0 and 2^40-1 and make
    sure that it is always the same random number by using "select * from". The resulting shifted IDs
    will still look similar if the original IDs looked similar, so we use a linear congruential generator
    as described on Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_congruential_generator
    where X_n+1 = (a * X_n + c ) mod m
    In our case m = 2^40 and we are trying to have m be the period, so we follow the rules for
    "m a power of 2, c != 0"
    The exact number of c is not critical except that is needs to be coprime with m, and we simply choose 1.
    For a we have the condition that a-1 needs to be divisible by 4 but also that a-1 needs to be
    divisible by all prime factors of m, which is automatically fulfilled by the former condition.
    Also, for a "good" random number generator, a should not be divisible by the prime factors of m more
    often that necessary, so in our case we want a-1 to be divisible by 4 but not by 8. To choose our
    value of a, I simply looked for large prime numbers and found 101111111111, but that is not one more
    than a multiple of 4, so I multiplied it by 3, giving us 303333333333. The prime factorization for
    this number minus 1 is 2^2 * 46141 * 1643513, so it should be a good value for a random number generator.
    These calculations will give large intermediary results, so we cast everything to decimal(24, 0).
    -->
    <sql>
      update person
      set human_readable_id = (
        (id + (select *
               from cast(floor(random() * 1099511627775) as decimal(24, 0)))) *
        303333333333:: decimal (24, 0) +
        1:: decimal (24, 0)) % 1099511627776:: decimal (24, 0)
      where human_readable_id is null and reference_person_id is null;
    </sql>
  </changeSet>
  <changeSet author="GA-Lotse" id="1744121614001-3">
    <addUniqueConstraint columnNames="human_readable_id"
      constraintName="person_human_readable_id_unique"
      tableName="person"/>
  </changeSet>
  <changeSet author="GA-Lotse" id="1744121614001-4">
    <sql>
      alter table person
        add constraint human_readable_id_not_null_for_reference_person
          check (num_nonnulls(reference_person_id, human_readable_id) >= 1)
    </sql>
  </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
